{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1789478988,"nonce":"vN+qqUm+4zOSQ2DeYTRFBdg8aGE=","target":"082b140c24527e39786e88eeacd27768d8e478863a09e4893019ff8761064514","message":"b4 has three different ways of turning an EmailMessage into bytes, and they\ndisagree about what the output should look like. We should unify them behind a\nsingle serializer.\n\nHow this came up\n----------------\n\nBrigham Campbell submitted a one-line patch to add the mbox \"From \" line to the\ninput we hand to the auto-to-cc commands:\n\n  [PATCH b4] ez: include mbox \"From \" line in auto-to-cc input\n  https://lore.kernel.org/tools/20260914-mbox-from-auto-to-cc-v1-1-fbf0c3fbd460@brighamcampbell.com\n  Message-ID: \u003c20260914-mbox-from-auto-to-cc-v1-1-fbf0c3fbd460@brighamcampbell.com\u003e\n\n    -        msgbytes = msg.as_bytes()\n    +        msgbytes = msg.as_bytes(unixfrom=True)\n\nThe patch itself is correct and worth taking. But it is a point fix for one\ncall site, and the reason that call site was wrong is the inconsistency\ndescribed below. The patch submission also asked, reasonably, why b4 formats\npatches \"by hand\" at all instead of shelling out to `git format-patch` -- see\n\"Why we do not use git format-patch\" at the end, which should become a docstring\nregardless of what we do with the rest of this.\n\nThe three serializers\n---------------------\n\n1. `LoreMessage.get_msg_as_bytes()` (b4/__init__.py) -- our own hand-rolled\n   serializer, added in a251bcb (\"Implement our own msg_to_bytes\") to dodge\n   header-encoding bugs in Python's email module that upstream never fixed. It\n   never emits a \"From \" line. Used by the mbox/maildir writers, by\n   `run_local_check()` (checkpatch and `prep-perpatch-check-cmd`), and by the\n   `needs-editing` preflight check.\n\n2. `msg.as_bytes(unixfrom=True, policy=b4.emlpolicy)` -- stdlib, in\n   `format_patch()` (ez.py). Emits the \"From \" line. This is the codepath a251bcb\n   declared unsafe for headers.\n\n3. `msg.as_bytes()` -- stdlib, no \"From \" line, in `auto_to_cc()` (ez.py). Dates\n   back to a2f81bd, the original `b4 submit` implementation, where the mbox write\n   a couple hundred lines further down did pass `unixfrom=True`. This looks like\n   an oversight rather than a decision; nothing in the history records a reason.\n\nSo today: patches written to disk get a \"From \" line, patches piped to the\nauto-to-cc commands do not, and patches piped to the per-patch check commands go\nthrough a third serializer that also does not. Whether an external command sees\nthe \"From \" line depends on which of b4's features invoked it.\n\nWhy this matters, with numbers\n------------------------------\n\nConfirmed by testing against a real prep branch:\n\n- The auto-to-cc commands currently receive no \"From \" line. `get_maintainer.pl`\n  does not care: To: and Cc: output is byte-identical with and without it, so\n  the overwhelming majority of users see no difference either way.\n- `contrib/contacts/git-contacts` from git.git does care. Its `scan_patches()`\n  only sets `$id` from `/^From ([0-9a-f]{40}) Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001$/` and\n  then does `next unless $id;`, so with no \"From \" line it emits nothing, exits\n  0, and b4 silently adds no addresses. That is the bug Brigham hit.\n  (Caveat for the archive: `git-contacts` never reads stdin -- it dies with \"No\n  input revisions or patch files\" unless given a file path or rev args -- so it\n  only works as an auto-to-cc command when configured as\n  `git contacts /dev/stdin`. git-send-email's --cc-cmd passes the patch file as\n  argv instead, which is why this works there and not here.)\n- `format_patch()` writes the cover letter with the stdlib fallback\n  `From nobody \u003cctime\u003e`, where `git format-patch --cover-letter` writes\n  `From \u003chash\u003e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001`. A separate small divergence in the one\n  codepath that already passes `unixfrom=True`.\n- Escaping is not a new hazard: neither `git format-patch` nor\n  `git show --format=email` escapes body lines beginning with \"From \", so adding\n  the header line does not make our output any less mbox-correct than git's.\n\nProposed refactor\n-----------------\n\nMake `LoreMessage.get_msg_as_bytes()` the single way b4 turns a message into\nbytes, and teach it the \"From \" line:\n\n- Add a parameter to `get_msg_as_bytes()` for the mbox \"From \" line, defaulting\n  to off. When asked for it, prefer the message's existing unixfrom (which\n  `git show --format=email` provides and our in-place mutations preserve) and\n  fall back to a deterministic sentinel rather than stdlib's\n  `From nobody \u003cctime\u003e`. `save_git_am_mbox()` already hand-writes such a\n  sentinel and should use the new parameter instead.\n- Convert `auto_to_cc()` and `format_patch()` off stdlib `as_bytes()`. This\n  fixes Brigham's bug, fixes the `From nobody` cover letter, and gets both off\n  the codepath a251bcb exists to avoid.\n- Decide deliberately whether `run_local_check()` should also emit the line.\n  checkpatch does not need it, but the decision should be recorded rather than\n  inherited from whichever serializer the call site happened to pick.\n- Add tests asserting the \"From \" line is present or absent per consumer, so\n  this cannot silently drift again.\n\nRisk\n----\n\nAny existing custom `send-auto-to-cmd` / `send-auto-cc-cmd` gains a new first\nline. `email.message_from_bytes` and `mailbox` handle it, `get_maintainer.pl`\nand `checkpatch.pl` are unaffected, but a script doing naive first-line parsing\nwould change behaviour. Low blast radius, worth a release note.\n\nWhy we do not use git format-patch\n----------------------------------\n\nRecording this because it gets asked, and because the history does not answer\nit. a2f81bd replaced `git_format_patches()` (tempdir plus\n`format-patch --cover-letter`) with `git_range_to_patches()`\n(`git show --format=email` per commit, parsed into an EmailMessage) because b4\nneeds mutable message objects, not files. It rebuilds Subject through\n`LoreSubject` (revision, prefixes, counter, presubject), injects deterministic\nMessage-IDs from the change-id template, derives every patch's Date from a\nsingle series timestamp, moves From into the body for `mailfrom`, adds\n`X-B4-Tracking` and `X-Change-ID`, threads the series, mixes in or adds the\ncover, and then hands the objects to patatt for signing and to the send path.\n`git format-patch` writes files with its own numbering, threading and\nMessage-IDs, all of which we would have to reparse and overwrite.\n\nThe tradeoff is that `git show` output has to be taught to match format-patch,\nwhich we have done incrementally: bbb711b (diffstat), 71916ed/08550da\n(`--default-prefix`), 8438e30/4253590/509f56f (mailmap). Brigham's patch is the\nnext item in exactly that series, which is a good argument that the divergences\nshould be closed in one place rather than one call site at a time.","files":null}]}